# garb NEON luma (RGB->gray) experiment — NEUTRAL, kernels NOT shipped
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cargo bench --features experimental --bench swizzle -- 'sweep_rgba_to_gray'
sizes:   64x64 / 256x256 / 1024x1024 / 4096x4096

## What was tried

rgb/bgr/rgba/bgra -> gray for all three matrices (bt709, bt601, bt2020),
contiguous and strided: vld3q_u8 / vld4q_u8 to deinterleave 16 pixels, then
vmulq_n_u16 + two vmlaq_n_u16 + vshrn_n_u16 for the weighted sum. Bit-exact
with scalar (u16 arithmetic, (r*wr + g*wg + b*wb + 128) >> 8; max accumulator
255*256 + 128 = 65408, so u16 cannot overflow). All 8 dispatch sites wired,
232 tests green across 25 archmage token permutations.

## Result — NEON vs scalar (median)

| size        | NEON      | scalar    | delta |
|-------------|-----------|-----------|-------|
| 64x64       |  453.03ns |  452.56ns |  +0.1%|
| 256x256     |   7.305us |   7.306us |   0.0%|
| 1024x1024   | 117.29us  | 117.31us  |   0.0%|
| 4096x4096   |   1.8948ms|   1.8934ms|  +0.1%|

Exactly neutral at every size. LLVM autovectorises the scalar luma loop
(a simple map over chunks_exact with u16 math) into essentially the same code.

## Dispatch was VERIFIED, not assumed

Numbers this identical are exactly what "the SIMD path never ran" looks like,
so it was checked rather than trusted: making `luma_combine` return
`vdupq_n_u8(7)` caused the probe to print 7 and failed 4 luma tests
(luma_roundtrip_identity, permutation_rgb_to_gray_bt709,
permutation_bgra_to_gray_bt709, permutation_rgba_to_gray_bt2020). The NEON
path is genuinely dispatched; the tie is real.

(A first probe that only perturbed the rounding bias 128->129 changed nothing,
because >>8 truncation absorbs it. A neutral-looking result from a weak probe
is not evidence — the probe has to actually be able to fail.)

## Decision: NOT shipped

Zero measured gain for ~200 lines of hand-written NEON is not worth the
maintenance and bit-exactness burden. Reverted, kept recoverable in git stash
and in this record.

## Worth revisiting on other ARM cores

This is a tie on Apple's very wide out-of-order cores with strong LLVM
autovectorisation. Cores where autovec is weaker or absent — Ampere/Graviton,
and especially in-order Cortex-A5x — could plausibly favour the explicit
kernel. That is a hypothesis, NOT a measurement: do not re-add these kernels
without running this same sweep on that hardware. The bench group
(`sweep_rgba_to_gray` in benches/swizzle.rs) is committed so the comparison is
one command away.
